          "type": "multipart",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "stem": "For the function \\( y = x^2(x - 6)^2 \\), complete the following steps.",
          "parts": [
            {
              "id": "y12a-3b-q10a",
              "type": "mc",
              "stem": "a) What is the derivative \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} \\) of \\( y = x^2(x - 6)^2 \\) using the product rule?",
              "options": [
                "\\( 4x(x - 6) \\)",
                "\\( 2x(x - 6)^2 \\)",
                "\\( 4x(x - 6)(x - 3) \\)",
                "\\( 2x(x - 6)(2x - 3) \\)"
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "Let \\( y = uv \\) where \\( u = x^2 \\) and \\( v = (x-6)^2 \\).",
                  "work": "\\begin{aligned} \\frac{du}{dx} &= 2x \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dx} &= 2(x-6) \\end{aligned}"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Apply the product rule: \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = u \\frac{dv}{dx} + v \\frac{du}{dx} \\).",
                  "work": "\\begin{aligned} \\frac{dy}{dx} &= x^2 \\cdot 2(x-6) + (x-6)^2 \\cdot 2x \\\\ &= 2x(x-6) [x + (x-6)] \\\\ &= 2x(x-6)(2x-6) \\\\ &= 4x(x-6)(x-3) \\end{aligned}"
                }
              ],
              "xp": 2
            },
            {
              "id": "y12a-3b-q10b",
              "type": "mc",
              "stem": "b) Which of the following correctly classifies the stationary points of the function?",
              "options": [
                "Local minimum at \\((0, 0)\\), local maximum at \\((6, 0)\\)",
                "Local minima at \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((6, 0)\\), local maximum at \\((3, 81)\\)",
                "Local minimum at \\((3, 81)\\) only",
                "Local maxima at \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((6, 0)\\), local minimum at \\((3, -81)\\)"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "Set the first derivative to zero to find the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the stationary points.",
                  "work": "4x(x-6)(x-3) = 0 \\implies x = 0,\\ x = 3,\\ \\text{or } x = 6"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Substitute these \\(x\\)-values back into the original function to find the corresponding \\(y\\)-coordinates.",
                  "work": "\\begin{aligned} y(0) &= 0^2(0-6)^2 = 0 \\implies (0, 0) \\\\ y(3) &= 3^2(3-6)^2 = 9(-3)^2 = 81 \\implies (3, 81) \\\\ y(6) &= 6^2(6-6)^2 = 0 \\implies (6, 0) \\end{aligned}"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Use a table of gradients to classify the nature of each stationary point.",
                  "work": "We observe the sign of \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c}\nx & -1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ \\hline\n\\frac{dy}{dx} & -112 & 0 & 40 & 0 & -32 & 0 & 112 \\\\\n\\text{Slope} & \\backslash & - & / & - & \\backslash & - & /\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFrom the table, we see:\n- At \\( x=0 \\): Slope changes from negative to positive \\(\\rightarrow\\) Local minimum at \\( (0,0) \\)\n- At \\( x=3 \\): Slope changes from positive to negative \\(\\rightarrow\\) Local maximum at \\( (3,81) \\)\n- At \\( x=6 \\): Slope changes from negative to positive \\(\\rightarrow\\) Local minimum at \\( (6,0) \\)"
                }
              ],
              "xp": 2
            },
            {
              "id": "y12a-3b-q10c",
              "type": "review",
              "stem": "c) Sketch the graph of the function, showing all key features.",
              "answer": "",
